---
import KafkaCompare from "../../components/KafkaCompare.astro";
import CaseLayout from "../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../content";

const item = projects.find((entry) => entry.id === "kona-token-trade");
if (!item) throw new Error("Kona Token Trade content is missing");
const sections = [
  { id: "problem", label: "The platform" },
  { id: "role", label: "My role" },
  { id: "kafka", label: "From calls to Kafka" },
  { id: "security", label: "Security and payments" },
  { id: "team", label: "Beyond the code" },
];
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Kona Token Trade · Nieb Hasan Neom"
  description={item.summary}
  heading="Kona Token Trade"
  lede="Backend services for a blockchain-based token-trading platform at Kona Software Lab, from its start as a P2P trading platform."
  sections={sections}
>
  <dl class="facts" slot="facts">
    <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Graduate Engineer, then Software Engineer II</dd></div>
    <div><dt>When</dt><dd>2022 – 2025</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Built with</dt><dd>Java 8, Spring Boot 2, Apache Kafka, PostgreSQL, MongoDB, AWS S3, SNS, EC2</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <section id="problem">
    <h2>The platform</h2>
    <p>
      I joined Kona Software Lab in 2022 as a Graduate Engineer, straight after graduation, on a P2P cryptocurrency trading platform. It grew into Kona Token Trade, a blockchain-based token-trading platform running on 30 Spring Boot microservices for trading, wallets, users, payments, and fees.
    </p>
    <p>A trade touches several of those services, so a slow or failing service anywhere in the chain can put a transaction in progress at risk.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="role">
    <h2>My role</h2>
    <p>
      I owned 6 of the 30 services and contributed to 15, working across the full development cycle on the trade, wallet, user, and fee-management systems. I was promoted to Software Engineer II in 2024.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kafka">
    <h2>From synchronous calls to Kafka</h2>
    <p>
      Services called each other over synchronous REST. A service would call the next one and wait for the reply, so if that downstream service was unavailable, a transaction already in progress could fail with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I replaced those calls with Kafka messaging. A service now publishes an event and moves on, and the downstream service processes the event when it is available. Kafka keeps the event until it is read, so a service that is restarting or briefly down delays its part of the work instead of failing the whole trade.
    </p>
    <KafkaCompare />
  </section>

  <section id="security">
    <h2>Security and payments</h2>
    <p>
      I added two-factor authentication to buy and sell operations, integrated bank-account and Kona card payment gateways, and added caching to reduce response latency. The services were built against AWS S3, SNS, and EC2.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="team">
    <h2>Beyond the code</h2>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>Ran learning sessions on backend development for new joiners, and mentored and onboarded two junior engineers.</li>
      <li>Helped organize the 2023 Kona coding contest, where my team placed first runner-up of 20.</li>
      <li>Moved on to the <a href="/projects/card-personalization/">Card Personalization System</a>, bringing Kona I's system from Seoul to Bangladesh.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
